# Copy to .env and fill in.  DO NOT commit .env — it is gitignored.
#
# ============================================================ SUPABASE ====
#
# Find these in: Supabase Dashboard -> your project -> Connect
#
# Supabase offers three connection strings. Prisma needs TWO of them, and
# they are NOT interchangeable:
#
#   DATABASE_URL  -> Transaction pooler, port 6543.
#                    Every runtime query. Safe for serverless (Vercel), where
#                    each invocation would otherwise open its own connection
#                    and exhaust the database.
#
#   DIRECT_URL    -> Direct or Session pooler, port 5432.
#                    Migrations only. `prisma migrate` issues DDL and advisory
#                    locks, which a transaction pooler cannot carry — pointing
#                    this at 6543 is the single most common cause of
#                    "prepared statement already exists" and hung migrations.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTE THE USERNAME DIFFERENCE. It trips up almost everyone:
#   pooled    -> postgres.[PROJECT-REF]      (username carries the project ref)
#   direct    -> postgres                    (plain)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Transaction pooler — port 6543. Note ?pgbouncer=true is REQUIRED:
# it tells Prisma to stop using prepared statements, which pgbouncer's
# transaction mode does not support.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.[PROJECT-REF]:[YOUR-PASSWORD]@aws-0-[REGION].pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1"

# Direct connection — port 5432.
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres:[YOUR-PASSWORD]@db.[PROJECT-REF].supabase.co:5432/postgres"

# If migrations fail with "Can't reach database server", your network is
# probably IPv4-only while Supabase's direct host is IPv6-only. Use the
# SESSION pooler instead — same port 5432, but pooler host and the
# project-ref username:
#
# DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.[PROJECT-REF]:[YOUR-PASSWORD]@aws-0-[REGION].pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"

# Percent-encode special characters in the password:
#   @ -> %40    # -> %23    ? -> %3F    / -> %2F    : -> %3A
# Or just reset it to something alphanumeric in Settings -> Database.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ON VERCEL: the Supabase integration does NOT create DATABASE_URL. It injects
# POSTGRES_PRISMA_URL / POSTGRES_URL / POSTGRES_URL_NON_POOLING instead, so a
# project relying on DATABASE_URL alone fails every query with
# "Environment variable not found: DATABASE_URL" — which the login route
# surfaces as "the account couldn't be looked up".
#
# src/lib/db/client.ts falls back through those names, so the integration's
# variables work as-is. Setting DATABASE_URL explicitly still takes precedence.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PREVIEW DEPLOYMENTS NEED THEIR OWN DATABASE.
#
# By default a Vercel project uses one set of variables for every environment,
# so preview deployments talk to the production database. That is not merely
# untidy — it has already broken production once. A preview build applied a
# migration renaming a column; production was still serving code generated
# from the old schema and its dashboard went down, from a branch nobody had
# merged. Preview also READS AND WRITES PRODUCTION ROWS: a test capture on a
# preview URL lands in the real table, next to real clients.
#
# scripts/migrate-deploy.mjs now refuses to migrate from a non-production
# build, which stops the schema half of that. The data half needs a second
# database — there is no code-side fix for it.
#
#   1. Create a second Supabase project. Call it something like
#      "sanas-salon-preview". The free tier allows two.
#
#   2. Point it at this schema:
#        DIRECT_URL="<preview project's 5432 URL>" npx prisma migrate deploy
#        DIRECT_URL="<...>" DATABASE_URL="<...>" npm run db:seed
#
#   3. In Vercel -> Settings -> Environment Variables, add DATABASE_URL and
#      DIRECT_URL with the preview project's values, ticking ONLY the
#      "Preview" environment. Leave the Production values alone.
#
#   4. Add ALLOW_PREVIEW_MIGRATIONS=1, Preview-only. Without it preview builds
#      keep skipping migrations, which is the safe default but means a preview
#      of a schema change cannot show the new column.
#
# Until step 4 is done, a preview of a schema change will report the database
# as unavailable on pages needing the new column. That is the guard working,
# not a fault — merge to main and production migrates itself.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ================================================================ AUTH ====

# Signs the session cookie. MUST be at least 32 characters — the app refuses
# to sign sessions in production without it, and login returns 500.
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
AUTH_SECRET=""

# Manager PIN a cashier must enter for discount overrides and invoice voids.
ADMIN_OVERRIDE_PIN=""


# ================================================================ SEED ====
# Consumed by `npm run db:seed` when creating the initial accounts.
# Leave a password blank and the seed generates a strong one and prints it
# once. Passwords are never stored in plaintext — only a PBKDF2 hash.
OWNER_EMAIL="owner@sanasbeauty.pk"
OWNER_PASSWORD=""
CASHIER_EMAIL="reception@sanasbeauty.pk"
CASHIER_PASSWORD=""
STAFF_EMAIL="ayesha@sanasbeauty.pk"
STAFF_PASSWORD=""
